 ## Background
-3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [S1, S3]. It builds on the concept of splatting, where 3D primitives are projected and rasterized to 2D. The method was developed to address the slow rendering speeds of NeRFs and the lack of explicit representation in neural fields. It has since become a standard for real-time neural rendering. Variants have been developed for text-to-3D [S1], denoising [S6], and large-scale scenes [S7].
+
+3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [6233-optimization-and-parallelization]. It builds on the concept of splatting, a technique used in computer graphics for rendering point sprites, and adapts it for differentiable rendering. The method was developed to address the slow rendering speeds of NeRF-based methods while maintaining high visual fidelity. It has since become a standard for real-time neural rendering [S1, S2].
 
